===Etymology===
Probably from Chinese [[鴨脚]] (yājiǎo) "duck feet" due to the shape of the leaves, the pronunciation then changing (along with the characters) to [[銀杏]] (yínxìng) "silver apricot". The same characters 銀杏 are used in Japanese (ichō) and Korean (eunhaeng). The Japanese characters used to write ginkgo look as though they could be read ginkyō, and this was the name Engelbert Kaempfer, the first Westerner to see the species in 1690, probably wrote ''gink'''j'''o'' in his Amoenitates Exoticae (1712).<ref>Wolfgang Michel, [http://catalog.lib.kyushu-u.ac.jp/handle/2324/2898/Ginkgo_biloba2_revised_2011.pdf ''On Engelbert Kaempfer’s “Ginkgo”'']</ref> However, his ''j'' was misread as a ''g'', and the misspelling stuck.

